  "body": "**All four scheduled workflows had never executed once.** Their crons fire on Mondays, the repositories were created on a Wednesday, and `workflow_dispatch` was enabled the whole time \u2014 so 26 of 53 job definitions had never been observed. I dispatched three of them. Eleven jobs failed. Four of the five hub failures are fixed here.\n\n## Windows could not check the repository out\n\nNot the lane \u2014 the checkout.\n\n```\nerror: invalid path 'fixtures/hostile/cases/nul.json'\n##[error]The process 'git.exe' failed with exit code 128\n```\n\n`nul` is a reserved device name on Windows. Git cannot create that file, so **every Windows claim this repository has ever made is about a checkout that never happened.** Renamed to `nul-character.json`, which is what the fixture was always about \u2014 a NUL character inside JSON, not a filename. Same bytes, same `CONTROL_CHARACTER_FORBIDDEN` reason code; generator and consumer both updated.\n\n## macOS could not install Python\n\n```\n##[error]The version '3.12.12' with architecture 'arm64' was not found for macOS 15.7.9\n```\n\nThe exact patch was never load-bearing: `ops/ci/rust-toolchain-boundary.sh` accepts any `^Python 3\\.12\\.[0-9]+$` and then verifies the resolved binary by digest. The pin is now the **3.12 series**, in the workflow and in the guard that asserts it \u2014 which still stops a silent jump to 3.13, the thing that guard is actually for.\n\n## Coverage never had a scanner\n\nThe coverage filter is the whole workspace, wider than the fast lane's partition, so it reaches `publication::scan::tests::actual_pinned_scanner_detects_binary_and_chunk_boundary_canaries`. That test **admits the pinned scanner rather than skipping**, so it panicked at `src/publication/scan_tests.rs:130`. The `fast` job already installs gitleaks for exactly this reason; now the coverage job does too.\n\n## The links lane had never installed its own tool\n\n```\n##[error]install-action: install-action does not support lychee@0.24.0\n```\n\n`taiki-e/install-action` does not carry lychee at the pinned revision \u2014 it never has. `ops/ci/install-lychee.sh` replaces it in the same shape as `ops/ci/install-gitleaks.sh`: one named asset over TLS, checked against the digest upstream publishes beside it, refuse rather than continue.\n\nVerified locally against the real documentation tree: **890 links, 889 OK, 0 errors.**\n\n## The audit job is deliberately left failing\n\n**Exit 78 is not neutral in GitHub Actions.** It never was \u2014 78 was the neutral code for the old Apps check API and has never meant anything to a workflow step. Every \"typed neutral refusal\" written on that pattern is a red job wearing a polite message, in all four repositories.\n\nIt cannot be repaired in the workflow, because `ops/ci/artifact-check.sh` requires an observation whose `tool_versions` names `jankurai`, and there is no version to record when the tool is absent. That is a contract four repositories share, so it is not mine to decide alone. `~/.bullet-orch-durable/CI-PORTABILITY-FINDINGS-20260910.md` sets out three options and my preference.\n\n## Also\n\nSix of the eight publication workflow digest pins are re-admitted from the published mains, clearing `PUBLICATION_CI_WORKFLOW_DIGEST_DRIFT`. Topology unchanged; only bytes moved.\n\nProved on the development host before pushing: the full `lint` lane green, the workflow policy green with the new guard text, and the renamed fixture green through both `bullet-contract check` and the `canonical_hostile` suite.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_015qHQABNS382h2gi5jb5oUY\n",
